WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.560
Hello and welcome back to JD Science.

2
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.520
Today, we are exploring monoclonal antibodies.

3
00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:10.940
These are identical antibodies made from a single parent cell.

4
00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:15.220
A mouse is injected with an antigen and its B lymphocytes

5
00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:17.260
produce antibodies against it.

6
00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:21.460
These lymphocytes are fused with tumor cells to make a hybridoma.

7
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.900
The hybridoma divides rapidly and produces large amounts

8
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.380
of identical antibodies.

9
00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:31.060
They can be used to locate molecules in cells, for

10
00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:33.980
pregnancy tests, and for testing hormone levels.

11
00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:37.780
In cancer treatment, they bind to cancer cells and

12
00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:40.260
tag them for attack by white blood cells.

13
00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:44.700
Compared with chemotherapy and radiotherapy, they target cells

14
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.380
more precisely and have fewer side effects.

15
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.620
A monoclonal antibody recognises a particular antigen or

16
00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:52.720
part of an antigen.

17
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.100
In traditional hybridoma production, an antibody producing B

18
00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:00.960
lymphocyte is fused with a tumor cell to combine specificity

19
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.500
with the ability to divide repeatedly.

20
00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:08.100
Monoclonal antibodies can be used in pregnancy tests, diagnostics

21
00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.220
and some targeted cancer treatments.

22
00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:13.120
They are not automatically free from side effects.

23
00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:16.740
Their benefits and risks depend on the particular treatment.

24
00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:20.720
As you revise this topic, try to explain the key process

25
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.000
in your own words.

26
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.500
Focus on what happens, why it happens and how the structures

27
00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.520
involved help the process work.

28
00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:32.620
Now pause the video and try this exam style

29
00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:33.180
question.

30
00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.280
Describe how a hybridoma cell is produced.

31
00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:37.880
Here is a model answer.

32
00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:42.100
A B lymphocyte from the mouse, which makes the antibody, is

33
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:43.700
fused with a tumor cell.

34
00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:47.900
The resulting hybrid provides rapidly and produces large

35
00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:49.780
amounts of identical antibodies.

36
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:54.240
If this explanation has helped, please subscribe to JDScience

37
00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.100
for more lessons.

38
00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:59.500
You can find further learning resources and practice questions at

39
00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:04.020
jdsciense .co

40
00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:04.740
.uk.

41
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.160
Thank you for watching.
